# Showcase notes, chapter 1. Drafted for Wisdom, reviewed: false until the owner checks REVIEW.md.
SHOW = {
'1.1': dict(
 notice="Janaka asks three things at once, and the whole text answers them in one stroke.",
 words=[("katham", "how"), ("jñānam", "knowledge, knowing the Self"), ("avāpnoti", "one gains"), ("muktiḥ", "freedom, liberation"), ("bhaviṣyati", "will come about"), ("vairāgyam", "dispassion, the cooling of craving"), ("prāptam", "reached"), ("etat", "this"), ("brūhi", "tell"), ("mama", "me"), ("prabho", "O lord, O master")],
 commentary="The text opens with a king asking a young teacher three plain questions. Janaka of Mithila already has everything a person can want, and tradition links this conversation to the Mahabharata story in which the boy Ashtavakra wins a debate at Janaka's court. So the questions come from someone who has tried the usual answers. He asks about knowledge, freedom and dispassion as if they were separate goals with separate methods. The traditional reading is that Ashtavakra answers all three at once. From the next verse on he treats the three as one fact seen from three sides: when you know what you are, you are free, and craving loses its grip on its own. That is why the Ashtavakra Gita feels so short on instructions. It skips the ladder. Commentators in the Advaita line read Janaka here as the ideal student described in the Upanishads: mature, dispassionate already, and ready to hear the conclusion first. For a reader today, the useful part is the honesty of the question. His question is about what is true, more than about how to feel better.",
 bridge=(4, 34, 'same', "Krishna tells Arjuna to learn from those who have seen the truth by asking them, and serving them."),
),
'1.2': dict(
 notice="The teacher's first answer is about conduct, before any talk of awareness.",
 words=[("muktim", "freedom"), ("icchasi", "you want"), ("cet", "if"), ("tāta", "my child, dear one"), ("viṣayān", "objects of the senses"), ("viṣavat", "like poison"), ("tyaja", "leave, give up"), ("kṣamā", "forgiveness, patience"), ("ārjava", "straightforwardness, honesty"), ("dayā", "kindness"), ("toṣa", "contentment"), ("satyam", "truth"), ("pīyūṣavat", "like nectar"), ("bhaja", "take in, keep to")],
 commentary="Ashtavakra calls the king tāta, the word a father uses for a child. Before he says anything about the Self, he gives an ethical instruction. Sense objects are to be left like poison, and five qualities are to be taken like nectar: forgiveness, honesty, kindness, contentment and truth. The image is precise. You do not argue with poison. You leave it in the cup. Nectar you drink. Traditional commentators note that the verse sets the ground for everything that follows. The later chapters say there is nothing to practise and nothing to give up, and readers who skip this verse sometimes take that as permission to drop ordinary decency. The Advaita tradition reads it the other way: a mind that keeps these five qualities becomes quiet enough to hear the teaching in 1.3 onward. Notice how ordinary the list is. Nothing exotic, nothing secret. Forgiveness and contentment are things you can practise at a family dinner or a difficult meeting today, and they are named here as the first step toward freedom.",
 bridge=(16, 2, 'same', "Krishna's list of the qualities of a gentle nature includes truth, kindness and freedom from greed."),
),
'1.3': dict(
 notice="Every element is named and set aside, and what remains is the one who sees them.",
 words=[("na", "not"), ("pṛthvī", "earth"), ("jalam", "water"), ("agniḥ", "fire"), ("vāyuḥ", "air"), ("dyauḥ", "sky, space"), ("bhavān", "you"), ("eṣām", "of these"), ("sākṣiṇam", "the witness"), ("ātmānam", "the Self"), ("cidrūpam", "whose form is awareness"), ("viddhi", "know"), ("muktaye", "for freedom")],
 commentary="Indian philosophy describes the body and the physical world as built from five elements: earth, water, fire, air and space. Ashtavakra lists all five and says you are none of them. This is the method the Upanishads call neti neti, not this, not this. You remove what can be seen until only the seer is left. The key word is sākṣin, the witness. A witness in a court watches events without taking part in them, and that is the sense here. The Advaita teachers point out that whatever you can observe, including your body, your moods and your thoughts, stands in front of you as an object. The one observing cannot be one of those objects. The verse then adds cidrūpa, whose form is awareness, so the witness has content of its own: the plain fact of being aware. Ashtavakra presents this as the means to freedom, muktaye. He asks Janaka to notice what was always the case, with nothing new to believe.",
 bridge=(13, 2, 'same', "Krishna calls the body the field and the one who knows it the knower of the field."),
),
'1.4': dict(
 notice="The verse promises freedom now, adhunā eva, with no waiting period.",
 words=[("yadi", "if"), ("deham", "the body"), ("pṛthak kṛtya", "setting apart"), ("citi", "in awareness"), ("viśrāmya", "resting"), ("tiṣṭhasi", "you remain"), ("adhunā eva", "right now"), ("sukhī", "happy"), ("śāntaḥ", "at peace"), ("bandhamuktaḥ", "free of bondage"), ("bhaviṣyasi", "you will be")],
 commentary="Two actions are named: set the body apart, and rest in awareness. Neither is a physical act. Setting the body apart means seeing it as something you have and use, the way you have a coat, instead of the thing you are. Resting in awareness means letting attention settle on the fact of being aware instead of running after each object. Then comes the startling phrase adhunā eva, right now. Most paths in the tradition speak of gradual purification over years or lifetimes. The Ashtavakra Gita, read by the Advaita teachers, says that freedom is the nature of the Self and so it cannot arrive later. It can only be recognised. The three results listed, happy, peaceful and unbound, are the same three things Janaka asked about in 1.1, in a different order. A reader trying this does not need a retreat. It can be done in a waiting room. Notice the body sitting there, notice that you are aware of it, and stay with the noticing for a few breaths.",
 bridge=(5, 21, 'same', "Krishna describes the happiness found within by one who is not attached to outer contacts."),
),
'1.5': dict(
 notice="Caste, stage of life and visible form are all named as things you are not.",
 words=[("tvam", "you"), ("vipra-ādikaḥ", "a Brahmin or any other"), ("varṇaḥ", "varna, social class"), ("āśramī", "one in a stage of life"), ("akṣa-gocaraḥ", "within reach of the eyes and senses"), ("asaṅgaḥ", "unattached"), ("asi", "you are"), ("nirākāraḥ", "formless"), ("viśva-sākṣī", "witness of everything"), ("sukhī bhava", "be happy")],
 commentary="The phrase vipra-ādiko varṇaḥ means a Brahmin or any of the other varnas. Ashtavakra is speaking to a Kshatriya king and tells him he is no varna at all. He adds that Janaka belongs to no āśrama, the four stages of student, householder, forest-dweller and renunciant, and that he is nothing the senses can grasp. These were the main ways a person was placed in society, and all of them belong to the body and its roles. The Advaita reading is consistent: such identities are real for social life and duty, and the Self they describe is untouched by them. The verse leaves dharma in place and says what you are underneath it. The teacher's own name sharpens the point. Ashtavakra means bent in eight places, and in the Mahabharata he is born with a body bent by his father's curse. Here that teacher tells a king that the body is not the person. The verse ends, as many do in this chapter, with a simple instruction: be happy.",
 bridge=(5, 18, 'same', "Krishna says the wise see the same Self in a learned Brahmin, a cow, an elephant, a dog and an outcaste."),
),
'1.6': dict(
 notice="Right and wrong are moved into the mind, along with pleasure and pain.",
 words=[("dharma-adharmau", "right and wrong, merit and demerit"), ("sukham", "pleasure"), ("duḥkham", "pain"), ("mānasāni", "belonging to the mind"), ("te", "yours"), ("vibho", "O all-pervading one"), ("kartā", "doer"), ("bhoktā", "enjoyer, the one who experiences results"), ("muktaḥ", "free"), ("eva", "indeed"), ("sarvadā", "always")],
 commentary="This verse sounds dangerous until you read it with the tradition. Dharma and adharma here mean merit and demerit, the moral weight that actions leave behind. Ashtavakra places them in the mind along with pleasure and pain. He calls Janaka vibhu, all-pervading, and says the Self is neither the doer of actions nor the one who reaps their results. Advaita teachers are careful with this. The body and mind still act, and they still answer for what they do. Ethics stays in force at the level where action happens. What changes is the sense of who is acting. The Self is compared to light in a room: everything in the room is seen by it, and none of it belongs to it. The phrase mukta eva asi sarvadā, you are always free, is the heart of the verse. Freedom is described as a fact about you, present before any effort, which is why the next verses can say that the only bondage is a mistake about who you are.",
 bridge=(13, 32, 'same', "Krishna says the Self, though dwelling in the body, does not act and is not stained."),
),
'1.7': dict(
 notice="Bondage is defined as one small error of seeing.",
 words=[("ekaḥ", "one, alone"), ("draṣṭā", "seer"), ("asi", "you are"), ("sarvasya", "of everything"), ("mukta-prāyaḥ", "free in effect, as good as free"), ("sarvadā", "always"), ("ayam eva", "this alone"), ("hi", "indeed"), ("te", "your"), ("bandhaḥ", "bondage"), ("draṣṭāram", "the seer"), ("paśyasi", "you see"), ("itaram", "as someone else")],
 commentary="Ashtavakra gives a definition of bondage that fits in half a line: you see the seer as someone other than yourself. In daily life we treat awareness as a property of a small person located behind the eyes. That person then seems to be one object among others, limited and exposed. The verse says this picture is the whole problem. There is one seer of everything, and it is what you are. The word mukta-prāya is unusual. Some commentators read it as free already, since prāya can mean in effect. Others read it as nearly free, meaning that the only thing between Janaka and freedom is this misreading. Both readings agree on the practical point. Nothing has to be built. One mistake has to be dropped. The Advaita teachers compare it to a man looking everywhere for a necklace he is wearing. When a friend points to his neck, he gains nothing new, and he stops searching. The verse invites the same kind of looking.",
 bridge=None,
),
'1.8': dict(
 notice="The thought 'I am the doer' is a snakebite, and the cure is something you drink.",
 words=[("aham kartā", "I am the doer"), ("iti", "thus"), ("ahaṃmāna", "the pride of 'I'"), ("mahā-kṛṣṇa-ahi", "great black snake"), ("daṃśitaḥ", "bitten"), ("na aham kartā", "I am not the doer"), ("viśvāsa", "trust, conviction"), ("amṛtam", "nectar, the drink of deathlessness"), ("pītvā", "having drunk"), ("sukhī bhava", "be happy")],
 commentary="A snakebite was the classic emergency in old India, and the black cobra the deadliest snake. Ashtavakra uses that picture for ahaṅkāra, the conviction that I am the one doing everything. The poison works slowly. Every success becomes my success, every failure my failure, and the mind is never at rest. The antidote is named viśvāsa, which means trust or firm conviction. It is the thought 'I am not the doer', held until it becomes natural. The Advaita commentators link this with the Bhagavad Gita, where actions are said to be done by the qualities of nature while the confused self thinks it is the doer. Passivity is not the aim. Work still gets done, often better, because the worker is not carrying the weight of being its only cause. Someone who has prepared well for an interview, and then lets the outcome be, knows a small version of this. The verse asks for that ease to be extended to everything.",
 bridge=(3, 27, 'same', "Krishna says actions are done by nature's qualities, and the one confused by ego thinks 'I am the doer'."),
),
'1.9': dict(
 notice="Certainty is the fire, and ignorance is a whole forest, not a single tree.",
 words=[("ekaḥ", "one"), ("viśuddha-bodhaḥ", "pure awareness"), ("aham", "I am"), ("niścaya", "certainty, firm conviction"), ("vahninā", "by the fire"), ("prajvālya", "having set ablaze"), ("ajñāna-gahanam", "the forest of not knowing"), ("vīta-śokaḥ", "free of grief"), ("sukhī bhava", "be happy")],
 commentary="Ignorance here is called gahana, a dense forest, the kind that is easy to get lost in and impossible to clear by hand. A fire clears it in one sweep. The fire is niścaya, a settled certainty: I am the one pure awareness. The tradition distinguishes between knowing something as information and knowing it with certainty. You can hear many times that you are awareness and still live as a frightened body. Niścaya is the point where the knowledge stops being an idea and becomes how you see. Advaita teachers say this certainty comes through hearing the teaching, reflecting on it until doubts are gone, and dwelling on it. Ashtavakra compresses all three into one image. The result named is vīta-śoka, free of grief. Grief is mentioned because it is the feeling that most tightly binds a person to the idea of being a limited self who can lose things. Burn the forest, and grief has nowhere to grow. The word niścaya returns many times in chapter 11.",
 bridge=(4, 37, 'same', "Krishna says the fire of knowledge burns all actions to ash, as a blazing fire burns firewood."),
),
'1.10': dict(
 notice="The world is compared to a snake seen on a rope, and the rope is you.",
 words=[("yatra", "in which"), ("viśvam idam", "this world"), ("bhāti", "appears, shines"), ("kalpitam", "imagined, projected"), ("rajju-sarpavat", "like a snake on a rope"), ("ānanda-paramānandaḥ", "joy, the highest joy"), ("saḥ", "that"), ("bodhaḥ", "awareness"), ("tvam", "you"), ("sukham", "happily"), ("bhava", "be")],
 commentary="The rope and the snake is the most famous example in Advaita. In dim light a coiled rope looks like a snake. The fear is real, the heartbeat is real, but nothing is there except rope. When a lamp is brought, the snake does not go anywhere. It was never there. Ashtavakra applies this to the world: it appears in awareness as the snake appears on the rope. The commentators are careful about what this claims. It allows the world its place in daily life and says that it has no being apart from the awareness in which it appears. The second half turns from the world to the reader. That awareness is ānanda, joy, and paramānanda, the highest joy, and you are it. So the instruction to be happy is less a command than a reminder. The verse pairs a large claim about the world with a personal one, and leaves the reader to notice that they are the same claim.",
 bridge=None,
),
'1.11': dict(
 notice="The famous line yā matiḥ sā gatiḥ is quoted as a saying the listener already knows.",
 words=[("mukta-abhimānī", "one who holds himself to be free"), ("muktaḥ", "free"), ("hi", "indeed"), ("baddhaḥ", "bound"), ("baddha-abhimānī", "one who holds himself to be bound"), ("api", "too"), ("kiṃvadantī", "a saying, a popular proverb"), ("iha", "here, in this world"), ("satyā", "true"), ("iyam", "this"), ("yā matiḥ", "whatever the conviction"), ("sā gatiḥ", "that is the outcome, the destination"), ("bhavet", "becomes")],
 commentary="This is the line most people quote from the Ashtavakra Gita, often without knowing its source. Ashtavakra presents the final phrase as a kiṃvadantī, a common saying, and says that here it holds true: yā matiḥ sā gatir bhavet, as your conviction, so your end. The first half applies it. One who takes himself to be free is free, and one who takes himself to be bound stays bound. Read quickly, this sounds like positive thinking. The tradition reads it more carefully. Mati here means the settled view of what you are, something deeper than a mood or an affirmation. Since the Self is already free, according to the previous verses, the only thing keeping a person bound is the view that he is bound. Change the view at its root and nothing else needs to change. Advaita teachers also use the verse as a warning. A person who keeps rehearsing his limitations, his failures and his bad luck builds a gati out of that mati. Where you settle your mind is where you end up living.",
 bridge=(17, 3, 'same', "Krishna says a person is made of his faith: as one's faith is, so one is."),
),
'1.12': dict(
 notice="Ten qualities of the Self are listed, and then one word explains why we miss them: bhramāt, through error.",
 words=[("ātmā", "the Self"), ("sākṣī", "witness"), ("vibhuḥ", "all-pervading"), ("pūrṇaḥ", "whole, full"), ("ekaḥ", "one"), ("muktaḥ", "free"), ("cit", "awareness"), ("akriyaḥ", "without action"), ("asaṅgaḥ", "unattached"), ("niḥspṛhaḥ", "without wanting"), ("śāntaḥ", "at peace"), ("bhramāt", "through error"), ("saṃsāravān iva", "as if caught in worldly life")],
 commentary="The verse is almost a list, and its rhythm is part of its meaning. The Self is the witness, all-pervading, whole, one, free, awareness, without action, unattached, without wanting, at peace. Each word answers a fear the ordinary self lives with. The small self feels incomplete, and the Self is pūrṇa, whole. It feels isolated, and the Self is vibhu, everywhere. It feels busy and pulled, and the Self is akriya and asaṅga, inactive and unattached. Then comes the hinge: bhramāt saṃsāravān iva, through error it seems to be caught in saṃsāra. The word iva, as if, carries the whole argument. In Advaita the Self was never bound and then rescued. The binding is an appearance, like the blue of the sky, which you can see clearly and which is not a property of space. Reading the list slowly, one word per breath, is a traditional way to use a verse like this. Each word is checked against your own experience before moving on.",
 bridge=(13, 23, 'same', "Krishna names the Supreme Self in the body as the witness, the one who permits and sustains."),
),
'1.13': dict(
 notice="The verse asks you to drop the idea that you are a reflection of awareness.",
 words=[("kūṭastham", "unchanging, like an anvil"), ("bodham", "awareness"), ("advaitam", "one without a second"), ("ātmānam", "the Self"), ("paribhāvaya", "meditate on, dwell on"), ("ābhāsaḥ aham", "I am a reflection"), ("bhramam", "error"), ("muktvā", "letting go"), ("bhāvam", "notion, feeling"), ("bāhyam", "outer"), ("atha", "and"), ("āntaram", "inner")],
 commentary="Kūṭastha is the anvil in a blacksmith's shop. Everything is hammered on it, and it does not change shape. Ashtavakra uses it for the Self: unchanging awareness, one without a second. The instruction is paribhāvaya, dwell on this repeatedly. Then he names the error to drop: ābhāso 'ham, I am a reflection. Some Advaita schools explain the individual self as awareness reflected in the mind, like the sun reflected in a pot of water. The reflection moves when the water moves, and seems small and shaky. Ashtavakra tells Janaka not to identify with the reflection. Take your stand as the sun instead of its image in the pot. He adds a second thing to drop: the sense of outer and inner. We usually feel that we live inside and the world is outside. If awareness is one without a second, that border is also a construction. This verse is unusual in chapter 1 because it gives a practice, paribhāvaya. Even the Ashtavakra Gita, which later dismisses practice, allows this much: keep returning to what you are.",
 bridge=(12, 3, 'same', "Krishna describes the imperishable as all-pervading, unthinkable, unchanging, kūṭastha, steady and fixed."),
),
'1.14': dict(
 notice="Ashtavakra calls the king putraka, little son, while handing him a sword.",
 words=[("deha-abhimāna", "the conviction 'I am the body'"), ("pāśena", "by the rope, the noose"), ("ciram", "for a long time"), ("baddhaḥ asi", "you have been bound"), ("putraka", "my son, dear child"), ("bodhaḥ aham", "I am awareness"), ("jñāna-khaḍgena", "with the sword of knowledge"), ("tat", "that"), ("nikṛtya", "cutting through"), ("sukhī bhava", "be happy")],
 commentary="The noose in this verse is dehābhimāna, the settled feeling that I am this body. It is old, ciram, and it is tight. Ashtavakra calls Janaka putraka, little son, which is tender and also a little funny given that the speaker is the younger of the two. Then he hands him a sword: the knowledge 'I am awareness'. A sword is used once, cleanly. You do not untie a noose knot by knot when you can cut it. The Advaita teachers read this as the same point made in 1.9 with a fire: knowledge acts all at once, not by degrees. The body still exists and still needs care. What the sword cuts is the identification that makes every ache a threat to me. The sword image also appears in the Bhagavad Gita, where Krishna tells Arjuna to cut doubt with the sword of knowledge and stand up. There the result is action. Here the result is happiness. Both are what follows when the noose is gone.",
 bridge=(4, 42, 'same', "Krishna tells Arjuna to cut the doubt in his heart with the sword of knowledge and stand up."),
),
'1.15': dict(
 notice="Practising samadhi is named as the bondage itself.",
 words=[("niḥsaṅgaḥ", "unattached"), ("niṣkriyaḥ", "without action"), ("asi", "you are"), ("sva-prakāśaḥ", "shining by your own light"), ("nirañjanaḥ", "without stain"), ("ayam eva", "this alone"), ("te bandhaḥ", "your bondage"), ("samādhim", "samadhi, meditative absorption"), ("anutiṣṭhati", "you keep practising"), ("hi", "indeed")],
 commentary="This is one of the verses that makes the Ashtavakra Gita different from most yoga texts. Samadhi, deep meditative absorption, is usually the goal of practice. Here Ashtavakra says your bondage is that you practise it. The reason is in the first line. You are unattached, actionless, self-luminous and stainless already. If you practise samadhi in order to become free, you have assumed you are not free, and the practice confirms the assumption every day. The commentators read this as a teaching for a particular kind of student, with no contempt for meditation, one like Janaka who is ready to hear the conclusion. For many people, practice is how the mind becomes calm enough to hear this at all, and the Bhagavad Gita says the mind is steadied by practice and dispassion. Ashtavakra is speaking about the last step, where the practitioner sees that the meditator, too, was an idea. The verse is a warning against turning freedom into a project with a finish line.",
 bridge=(6, 35, 'contrast', "Krishna tells Arjuna the restless mind is steadied by practice and dispassion, the step this verse assumes is behind you."),
),
'1.16': dict(
 notice="The world is said to be strung on you, the word used for beads on a thread.",
 words=[("tvayā", "by you"), ("vyāptam", "pervaded"), ("idam viśvam", "this world"), ("tvayi", "in you"), ("protam", "strung, woven"), ("yathārthataḥ", "in truth"), ("śuddha-buddha-svarūpaḥ", "whose nature is pure awareness"), ("tvam", "you"), ("mā gamaḥ", "do not go into"), ("kṣudra-cittatām", "smallness of mind")],
 commentary="Prota means strung, as beads are strung on a thread, or woven, as threads run through cloth. Ashtavakra tells Janaka that the world is pervaded by him and strung on him. The thread does not show among the beads, yet nothing holds together without it. Then the verse turns personal: do not fall into kṣudra-cittatā, smallness of mind. The tradition reads this as the practical consequence of the teaching. If the whole world is strung on awareness, then pettiness, grudges and anxious self-concern are a strange way for awareness to behave. It is like a king worrying about a coin. The verse prescribes no effort to become large-minded. It reminds Janaka that he already is, and asks him not to forget it. The same image of beads on a thread appears in the Bhagavad Gita, where Krishna says all this is strung on him like pearls on a string. The two texts use one picture for one claim, from two different speakers.",
 bridge=(7, 7, 'same', "Krishna says all this is strung on him like pearls on a thread."),
),
'1.17': dict(
 notice="The verse describes a person you could meet: cool, deep, unshaken.",
 words=[("nirapekṣaḥ", "needing nothing"), ("nirvikāraḥ", "unchanging"), ("nirbharaḥ", "full, solid"), ("śītala-āśayaḥ", "cool at heart"), ("agādha-buddhiḥ", "of fathomless understanding"), ("akṣubdhaḥ", "unshaken"), ("bhava", "be"), ("cinmātra-vāsanaḥ", "with your one leaning toward awareness alone")],
 commentary="Most of chapter 1 describes the Self. This verse describes how a person who knows it carries himself. Nirapekṣa means not depending on anything. Nirbhara means full, without hollow places that need filling. Śītalāśaya, cool at heart, is a lovely phrase in a hot country. It means the inner temperature stays even when things heat up. Agādha-buddhi, fathomless understanding, suggests deep water: you cannot touch the bottom with a stick. Akṣubdha, unshaken, is the word for a calm sea. The last phrase is the key to the others. Vāsanā usually means the habit-tendencies that pull the mind back to the same desires. Here Ashtavakra uses it in a good sense: let your one habit be awareness alone. The commentators read this as a concession to how minds work. Minds will lean somewhere, so let them lean toward their source. The portrait resembles the steady-minded person of the Bhagavad Gita's second chapter, who is neither shaken by sorrow nor hungry for pleasure.",
 bridge=(2, 56, 'same', "Krishna describes the one of steady wisdom, unshaken in sorrow and free of craving in pleasure."),
),
'1.18': dict(
 notice="The verse sorts all things into two kinds, with form and without, and says which one is real.",
 words=[("sākāram", "with form"), ("anṛtam", "unreal, untrue"), ("viddhi", "know"), ("nirākāram", "without form"), ("tu", "but, while"), ("niścalam", "unmoving, steady"), ("etat", "this"), ("tattva-upadeśena", "by teaching on what is real"), ("na punaḥ", "not again"), ("bhava-sambhavaḥ", "coming into existence, rebirth")],
 commentary="Everything with a shape changes. The body grows and ages, mountains wear down, even stars burn out. Ashtavakra says what has form is anṛta, untrue, in the sense that it does not stay what it is. What has no form is niścala, unmoving. The Advaita reading uses a test from the Upanishads: the real is what does not change across past, present and future. By that test, forms fail and awareness passes, since it is present in waking, dream and deep sleep alike. The second line makes a large promise. Through this tattvopadeśa, teaching on what is real, there is no more coming into birth. The commentators explain that rebirth in this tradition follows identification with form. If you take yourself to be the formless, there is nothing to carry from one form to the next. A reader does not need to settle questions about rebirth to use the verse. Its first line is a lens for any loss. What has form was always going to change. What sees the change was never in danger.",
 bridge=(2, 16, 'same', "Krishna says the unreal has no being and the real never ceases to be."),
),
'1.19': dict(
 notice="The mirror is both inside the reflection and around it, and so is the Lord with the body.",
 words=[("yathā eva", "just as"), ("ādarśa", "mirror"), ("madhya-sthe", "standing within"), ("rūpe", "in the image, the form"), ("antaḥ", "inside"), ("paritaḥ", "all around"), ("tu", "and"), ("saḥ", "it"), ("tathā eva", "in the same way"), ("asmin śarīre", "in this body"), ("parameśvaraḥ", "the Supreme Lord")],
 commentary="Look at a face in a mirror. Where is the mirror? It is behind every point of the image, and it extends past the edges of the image too. The face seems to have depth and to be somewhere, but every part of it is mirror. Ashtavakra says the Supreme Lord is in this body the same way, inside it and all around it. The word chosen is parameśvara, the Supreme Lord, which shows that the Ashtavakra Gita, for all its austerity, does not avoid the language of devotion. The Advaita teachers read the mirror as awareness and the face as the body. The body seems solid and separate, but there is no point in it where awareness is absent, and awareness does not stop at the skin. A practical way to use this verse is to feel the body from inside for a moment, the breath and the weight, and then notice that the knowing of these sensations has no edge. The image is inside the mirror. The mirror is not inside the image.",
 bridge=(9, 4, 'same', "Krishna says all this is pervaded by him in his unmanifest form, and all beings rest in him."),
),
'1.20': dict(
 notice="Break the pot and the space inside does not go anywhere.",
 words=[("ekam", "one"), ("sarva-gatam", "everywhere"), ("vyoma", "space, sky"), ("bahiḥ", "outside"), ("antaḥ", "inside"), ("yathā", "just as"), ("ghaṭe", "in a pot"), ("nityam", "eternal"), ("nirantaram", "unbroken, without gaps"), ("brahma", "Brahman"), ("sarva-bhūta-gaṇe", "in every host of beings"), ("tathā", "so")],
 commentary="The pot and the space is the second classic example in Advaita, alongside the rope and the snake. A clay pot seems to enclose a little space of its own. But the space inside and outside is one space, and when the pot breaks, nothing moves. The pot-space was never a separate thing. Ashtavakra applies this to Brahman, present in every being. Each body seems to hold a private self, and Brahman is one, eternal and nirantara, without gaps, inside and outside every creature. Chapter 1 ends here, having moved from Janaka's three questions to a picture of the whole of reality. The tradition draws two lessons from the verse. The first concerns death: when the pot breaks, the space is not harmed. The second concerns other people: the space in your pot and the space in your neighbour's pot are the same space. The Bhagavad Gita uses a related image, of the wind that moves everywhere and still rests in space, to say how all beings rest in the Lord.",
 bridge=(9, 6, 'same', "Krishna compares all beings resting in him to the great wind that moves everywhere and stays within space."),
),
}
